{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789243517,"nonce":"b0/7iqDubh59E3gzAdfGYHldwwU=","title":"Python classes: what the struct + dispatch model does not cover yet","message":"Classes lower to reference structs with the class index as the first\nfield, methods to `Class$m` functions, overrides to tag-switch\ndispatchers, dynamic receivers to per-name dispatchers, instances boxed\nunder a kind per class. Not covered:\n\n1. Class attributes and static/class methods, properties, `__slots__`,\n   multiple inheritance, metaclasses.\n2. A method used as a value (`f = obj.method`) or a class used as a\n   value (`cls = Point; cls(1, 2)`).\n3. Dunders beyond `__init__`, `__str__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__` and the\n   ordering and arithmetic operators with a typed left operand:\n   `__getitem__`, `__contains__`, `__iter__`, `__hash__`, reflected\n   operators, and any dunder reached through a dynamic left operand.\n4. `isinstance` with a tuple of types; `type(obj)` prints the bare class\n   name rather than `\u003cclass '__main__.Name'\u003e`.\n5. Field types are the join of every assignment; a field assigned an int\n   in one method and a str in another is dynamic, with a box on each\n   store. Instances and containers in fields are always boxed.","files":null}]}